1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,000
One command from deleting a production database.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Real recording, live install. One command, local mode, no account.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
2025: an agent at Replit deleted a production database during a code freeze. This is the recreation.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:27,000
A recovery runbook says the standby is verified — step two deletes the corrupted primary.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,300
The agent is careful. It checks the runbook. It even wants to verify the standby itself.

6
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:45,000
Then it does what the runbook says. Not a malicious agent — a convinced one.

7
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:50,500
Its very first gcloud call dies at the hook — it never reaches the delete. Policy: gcloud is deny.

8
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,500
A rule that holds even when the story is good. No database was touched.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Same session, normal work: the tests run, unprompted.

10
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:08,300
The receipt: the exact command, classified, denied — source: policy.

11
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:15,000
One honest caveat: this gates the action. Scope the credential provider-side too.

12
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:18,000
Full write-up linked below. One command to try it.

